        "description": "<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"\" data-caption=\"\" data-portal-copyright=\"\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/03/55146110863_3ec9504855_o.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\t\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"has-drop-cap has-text-align-none\">AI was everywhere at the GDC Festival of Gaming this year. Vendors at the event pitched generative AI tools for things like making <a href=\"https://www.artificial.agency/\">AI-driven NPCs</a> and <a href=\"https://tesana.ai/\">even entire games from a chat box</a>. On the show floor, I spent 10 minutes playing a demo of a pixel-art fantasy world generated by Tencent's AI tools. In a briefing with Razer, I watched an AI assistant for QA automatically log issues in a shooter game. And there were <em>many</em> talks about AI, including <a href=\"https://www.gamefile.news/p/google-deepmind-genie-3-gdc\">a standing-room only presentation</a> by Google DeepMind researcher",
